Q: Cancel file download with http response in Flutter A: <p>Canceling a request without closing the client.<br />
Closing the client is not the best way to cancel a request.</p>
<pre class="lang-dart prettyprint-override"><code>import 'package:cancelable_http_client/cancelable_http_client.dart';
import 'package:http/http.dart';

Future&lt;void&gt; main() async {
  const timeout = 10;
  final cts = CancellationTokenSource();
  final token = cts.token;
  final client = CancelableClient(token);
  cts.cancelAfter(Duration(milliseconds: timeout));
  Response? resp;
  try {
    resp = await client.get(Uri.parse('https://pub.dev/feed.atom'));
    // Cancel the timer that schedules the cancellation request.
    // Not necessary. But why do we need an unnecessary active timer?
    cts.cancelAfter(null);
  } on TaskCanceledException {
    print(&quot;Canceled after $timeout ms&quot;);
  }

  if (resp != null) {
    if (resp.statusCode != 200) {
      throw StateError('HTTP error (${resp.statusCode})');
    }

    print(resp.body);
  }
}

</code></pre>
<p>Results:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>Canceled
</code></pre>

Q: (!) Some chunks are larger than 500 KiB after minification A: <p>Update your <strong>vite.config.js</strong>:</p>
<pre><code>export default defineConfig({
    ...,
    build: {
        chunkSizeWarningLimit: 1600
    }
});
</code></pre>
<p>It will bump up the warning size to 1.6MB which is still a very safe value. Even 2MB would not be a realy problem with todays networks.</p>
